.TH CVEC_CONSTRUCT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_construct_mp \- declare and create a vector with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_construct_mp(v, type, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable named v that holds elements of the
given type, and creates the vector in the same step. The vector takes
every allocation that it makes from mprocs. The macro is cvec_declare(3)
followed by cvec_init_mp(3), and everything that those two macros document
holds here unchanged: it declares the companion type variables in the same
way, the other type-dispatching macros read them, and a failed creation
stops the program through ccol_fatal_err().

The vector keeps a private copy of the procedure table, so the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t object here does not need to live longer than the
vector, although the functions in that table do. When the vector is
destroyed, it frees the backing buffer, the copy of the table and the
container itself through those functions. A NULL mprocs is the same as a
call to cvec_construct(3).

The new vector holds no elements and starts at the minimum capacity of 4.
The capacity doubles when a push finds the buffer full and halves when the
element count falls below a quarter of it, but it never falls below that
minimum of 4.

Destroy the vector with cvec_destroy(3), or use
cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3), which destroys the vector automatically at the
end of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable to declare and create
type    Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations and then a statement, not to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector, with a message that names v and the error below it.
To handle a failed creation yourself, use cvector_create_full(3)
directly; that function does not stop the program.
The macro rejects a procedure table with any of its four members left as
NULL, and that rejection is one of the failures that stops the program
here.
Because the macro expands to declarations and then a statement, it
belongs where declarations are legal and cannot be the body of an if or
a loop without braces.
The macro pastes v into the names of the companion variables, so v must
be a plain identifier.
The companion type variables must stay in scope everywhere that you use a
type-dispatching macro on v. For a handle that comes from another scope,
cvec_redeclare(3) makes them again.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

cvec_construct_mp(my_vec, int, &procs);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_redeclare (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3),
.BR cvector_create_full (3)
